<attribute name="sectionName" nodeId="69683c80"></attribute>
<attribute name="sectionDescription" nodeId="43008c1">&lt;p&gt;
    Cost estimates typically include the resources and capabilities (people, physical resources, and funding) needed to
    deliver and sustain the program/project and associated benefits. Some examples of program/project costs include
    severance payments, relocation, third party costs, facilities spend, and software licenses.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Cost estimates need to cover all costs associated with the change process, including all internal and external costs
    associated with implementing the change over the benefit realization period. All costs carried by the organization are
    included (both direct and indirect), and it is important to consider whether benefits in one part of the organization
    result in additional costs elsewhere. Costs should be separated into capital and operating expenditure as these have
    separate impacts on the income statement and balance sheet.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Additionally, the assumptions behind the costs should also be documented and validated with key stakeholders.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
